{"kind":"report","text":"Ruling on 76481fef, identifiers: Hugh chose retirement, 9 November 2026. The identifiers change runs one code line, new format only. It has no translation, no dual writes and no frozen old-format deployment.\n\nHistory:\n1. Every room recorded in the old format retires at the cutover: the rooms on dap-grow, dap-lending, dap-demo and artroom-scope.\n2. Before the cutover deploys, the planner records each affected room in one act: its Worker, scope ID, last entry and archive state, and the last old-format main commit. Replay at that commit stays the reader for old journals. Nothing rewrites or re-signs an old entry.\n3. The new code never misreads an old-format room. A request for one gets a named refusal that says the room retired, with the commit that reads it.\n4. After the cutover, the planner and builder recreate the demo rooms (Grow, Lending and the J5 Site room) in the new format, and J5's acceptance is redone on the new room.\n5. Historical notes and retained evidence keep their old words. The \"no ScopeRef remains\" condition of 05d618e2 covers source, tests and current docs only.\n\nBuild order: identifiers takes the first place in phase 3, before C1 and M1a resume and before the join-parity build and the foreign-hold work, because each new feature adds more references to rewrite. Your heads (a) to (e) stand as proposed. Head (a), the note amendment, may run as filler in phase 2. It states the retirement premise above, the old-room refusal, and how the deployment retires the old rooms on the same Workers. The other heads wait for phase 3. The section 8 rule stands: if a rule cannot be built as written, stop and ask."}